WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.520
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" is blowing&nbsp;
up movie theaters like Rocket and one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.225
moon-destroying weapons. And there are plenty&nbsp;
of very good reasons for its deserved success.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.580
One of the biggest factors that weighed&nbsp;
on the release of "Ant-Man and The Wasp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.260
Quantumania" was the fact that it hit a wall of&nbsp;
negative critical reviews before it even reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.820
its opening night. In contrast, "Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy Vol. 3" was buzzing the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.264
the early screenings started. To be clear, some&nbsp;
reviews were a mixed bag. But that didn't matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.264 --> 00:00:34.964
The overwhelming message from early viewers was&nbsp;
simple: Vol. 3 is one of the MCU's crown jewels.

00:00:34.964 --> 00:00:39.900
The warm early reception paved the way for viewers&nbsp;
to flock to the cinemas to see if the critics were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.760
right. With so much buzz over the long-awaited&nbsp;
third installment, the strong initial response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.646
confirmed that everyone needed to get to a&nbsp;
theater to see the space spectacle for themselves.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.120
There's one significant external&nbsp;
factor that guaranteed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.780
third "Guardians" film would succeed at&nbsp;
the box office: There was very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.400
competition. James Gunn's third and final&nbsp;
MCU installment arrived in cinemas with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.029
evergreen movies populating screens and&nbsp;
no major tentpole film on the horizon.

00:01:07.029 --> 00:01:11.220
"Guardians of the Galaxy: Vol. 3" came out&nbsp;
on May 5th. "The Super Mario Bros. Movie"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.804
came out on April 5th. Despite&nbsp;
release dates one month apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.804 --> 00:01:19.500
"Super Mario" was still the runner-up at the box&nbsp;
office on Quill and company's opening weekend.

00:01:19.652 --> 00:01:22.472
"Look at us. We're adorable!"

00:01:22.554 --> 00:01:25.800
The pair was distantly followed&nbsp;
by other less popular options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.000
like "Evil Dead Rise," "Are You There God?&nbsp;
It's Me, Margaret," and newcomer "Love Again."

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.820
Other cinematic runs were also still playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.580
including "John Wick: Chapter&nbsp;
4" and "Dungeons and Dragons:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:41.289
Honor Among Thieves," which goes to show how slim&nbsp;
the options were on the first weekend of May 2023.

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:45.180
And the second-place competition from&nbsp;
the still-overperforming "Mario" only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:51.960
brought in $18.6 million, less than a sixth of the&nbsp;
galactic-sized total domestic haul of "Guardians."

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.700
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" came&nbsp;
out in early May, right in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.200
of spring. But the trailers billed the film&nbsp;
as an early summer blockbuster. The truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.640
summer blockbusters don't care about hard&nbsp;
calendar dates. Warm weather is here. Summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.720
break is right around the corner. Vacations&nbsp;
are on the calendar. The summer movie season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.806
has arrived, and the third "Guardians"&nbsp;
installment capitalized on that fact.

00:02:13.806 --> 00:02:18.000
The argument goes back and forth about whether the&nbsp;
summer box office is all it's cracked up to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.440
and the 2023 summer slate is certainly a&nbsp;
mixed bag. There are plenty of tentpole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.040
films and indie alternatives coming to&nbsp;
theaters in the coming months, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.960
post-pandemic theatrical slump continues to dog&nbsp;
cinemas, making any summer success questionable.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.740
Still, "Guardians 3" managed to pull&nbsp;
some of that summer box office magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.520
into Spring. The result was a film&nbsp;
billed as kicking off the supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.780
impending "summer swell," which certainly&nbsp;
didn't hurt the movie's must-see appeal.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.520
The theatrical experience isn't what&nbsp;
it used to be. Before the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.360
going to the movies was a common activity&nbsp;
regardless of what movie titles were up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.160
the marquee. But the entire pastime came to a&nbsp;
screeching halt in 2020 due to the pandemic.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.520
Since quarantines and social&nbsp;
distancing requirements have lifted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.200
theaters have had a slow and painful return&nbsp;
to normalcy. Even then, the "new normal"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.760
for theaters is hardly reminiscent of the&nbsp;
old one. Packed auditoriums are rare and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.486
only tend to happen when there is a truly&nbsp;
big movie that will attract an audience.

00:03:12.486 --> 00:03:16.980
"Guardians of the Galaxy: Vol. 3" fits the&nbsp;
bill of a genuine tentpole film in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:22.140
few others can. It's a large-scale story with&nbsp;
A-list talent and glittering special effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.780
that concludes the story of a top property in an&nbsp;
already wildly successful cinematic universe. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.760
post-pandemic theatrical standards, "Guardians of&nbsp;
the Galaxy: Vol. 3" was simply too big to fail.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.220
"Guardians of the Galaxy: Vol. 3" is worth&nbsp;
watching for the emotional story it tells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.720
But plenty of external factors had viewers&nbsp;
ready to watch it as soon as possible. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.484
of the most prominent of these was&nbsp;
watching James Gunn's MCU swan song.

00:03:46.484 --> 00:03:51.120
As the veteran director prepares to reboot&nbsp;
Superman as part of his DC-focused future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.065
fans were ready to flock to see his final&nbsp;
directorial moments in Marvel's universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.065 --> 00:03:59.580
The emotions were even higher for fans&nbsp;
who remembered Gunn's sudden dismissal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.466
from the franchise back in 2018 and his&nbsp;
even more abrupt rehiring two years later.

00:04:04.466 --> 00:04:07.680
Since Gunn's reinstatement, fans&nbsp;
have anxiously waited to see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.880
he would cook up for his last hurrah&nbsp;
in the MCU, and he didn't disappoint.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:18.300
Back in 2014, the unexpectedly emotional story&nbsp;
of the first "Guardians of the Galaxy" captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.320
the hearts of diehard and fairweather&nbsp;
fans alike. The success of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.220
film was so great that it competed with&nbsp;
obvious blockbusters like "The Avengers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.980
in the hearts and minds of MCU audiences. Its&nbsp;
sequel, while less popular, still built on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.404
existing narrative, simultaneously creating&nbsp;
more laughs and greater emotional stakes.

00:04:36.404 --> 00:04:40.620
"We were always searching for a&nbsp;
family until we found each other."

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:47.220
During the extended lull between the second and&nbsp;
third franchise films, the bumbling-but-heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:52.200
group got tangled up in the "Infinity War" affair&nbsp;
with the Avengers, where they lost Gamora and saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.700
half of their crew snapped into thin air. They&nbsp;
went through even more harrowing adventures in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.244
"Avengers: Endgame" before making a brief&nbsp;
pit stop in Thor's fourth franchise film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.244 --> 00:05:06.524
Then they set the stage for "Vol. 3" with the&nbsp;
Disney+ Christmas special released in late 2022.

00:05:06.524 --> 00:05:10.080
From their spunky origins to a&nbsp;
Christmas-light-infused romp on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.000
Knowhere with Kevin Bacon, this ragtag team&nbsp;
of heroes has seen it all. The fact that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.680
third standalone film was heavily billed as the&nbsp;
original group's last outing and the final chapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.863
of their story made it an automatic trip to the&nbsp;
theater on opening weekend for countless fans.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.720
The MCU is in the midst of a seismic shift. The&nbsp;
first three phases of the cinematic universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.980
collectively billed as the "Infinity Saga,"&nbsp;
officially ended in the wake of the conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.780
with Thanos. Since then, Phase 4 struggled to&nbsp;
recapture the magic of the first wave of Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.360
movies. New heroes were introduced in isolated&nbsp;
stories that only occasionally overlapped with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.340
larger narrative. In addition, the use of Disney+&nbsp;
limited series alongside full-length feature films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.260
disrupted and accelerated the traditionally&nbsp;
slow build-up of past MCU storytelling.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:01.140
Phase 5 has begun, with Kang looming and many&nbsp;
crossovers in store. Yet the transition from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.180
classic Avengers to a new roster of heroes remains&nbsp;
shaky. That’s why a group like the Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:11.700
the Galaxy is so priceless. The team links to the&nbsp;
past and projects into the future. A movie like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:16.645
this one evokes nostalgia while also setting&nbsp;
up the storylines and stakes of future films.

00:06:16.645 --> 00:06:21.360
This isn't just helpful behind the scenes.&nbsp;
Fans feel it too. The ability to live in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.620
comfort zone of past franchise experiences&nbsp;
while getting amped for future possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.568
gave "Vol. 3" a dual attraction that is&nbsp;
hard to find in the MCU at the moment.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.180
One morbidly fascinating factor that doubtless&nbsp;
drew many viewers to see "Guardians of the Galaxy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.266
Vol. 3" on opening weekend was the&nbsp;
question of who was going to die.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:45.000
While there are multiple death scares,&nbsp;
particularly for Star-Lord and Rocket, by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.800
the credits roll no one from the original gang is&nbsp;
given their final last words. Yet the question of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.206
which of the original gang would die was in the&nbsp;
air for months leading up to the film's release.

00:06:54.206 --> 00:06:58.980
The trailers showed clips of Nebula carrying&nbsp;
a limp Star-Lord. Rocket is shown surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.180
by white light. There's a snapshot of Quill&nbsp;
screaming in grief. The signs were there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.140
and while they ended up being red herrings,&nbsp;
they motivated a lot of viewers to get off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.220
the couch and hightail it to the nearest&nbsp;
big screen before they saw spoilers online.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.020
Rocket is one of the original Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy. He's been with the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.460
from the beginning when the crew bumped into&nbsp;
one another on Xandar. He bonded with his new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:25.080
family and suffered through losing them in The&nbsp;
Blip. Yet despite his presence, cynical humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:30.000
and endless devotion to his friends, we didn't&nbsp;
really know who the MCU's version of Rocket was.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.537
"Or if you want to blow up moons."

00:07:31.537 --> 00:07:33.106
"No one is blowing up moons."

00:07:33.106 --> 00:07:35.429
"You just wanna suck the joy out of everything."

00:07:35.429 --> 00:07:39.900
The promotional material made it clear that the&nbsp;
third "Guardians" movie wasn't just a send-off for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:44.700
the original team, but also an homage to Rocket.&nbsp;
The raccoon was an underdeveloped character that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.320
was near and dear to the director's heart. In a&nbsp;
2022 interview with "Entertainment Weekly," Gunn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.820
explained his motivation for shining a spotlight&nbsp;
on the Guardians' smartest member. He said,

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:58.080
"One of the reasons why I came back to [make]&nbsp;
this movie was because I felt like I needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.580
tell Rocket's story. I would've been very sad&nbsp;
not to complete the trilogy for many reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.040
but I just feel very connected to Rocket. I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.527
like nobody would be able to tell&nbsp;
his full story if it wasn't me."

00:08:08.527 --> 00:08:12.900
Gunn may have a special affinity for Rocket,&nbsp;
but there are millions of fans close behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.920
him. Learning the origins of everyone's&nbsp;
favorite Trash Panda was too good to miss.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.740
Groot has had one of the most transformative&nbsp;
journeys of any MCU character. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.940
stoic nature of Groot's race has remained intact&nbsp;
throughout the arc of his character's evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.760
the rest of him has changed&nbsp;
every time we've seen him.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:35.220
"I am Groot!"

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.300
The original Groot from "Guardians of the&nbsp;
Galaxy" went down in a self-sacrificing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.800
ball of arboreal glory as the team saved&nbsp;
Xandar from an Infinity Stone-wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.480
Ronan the Accuser. By the end of the film, an&nbsp;
offshoot of the hero was growing in a planter.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.280
In "Vol. 2," Baby Groot is dancing around&nbsp;
with toddler-like innocence. "Infinity War"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.324
features a teenage Groot obsessed with himself&nbsp;
and uninterested in helping unless he has to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.324 --> 00:09:01.842
By the "Guardians of the Galaxy Holiday&nbsp;
Special," Groot is looking all grown up.

00:09:01.842 --> 00:09:06.360
One of the small-yet-significant draws of a third&nbsp;
"Guardians of the Galaxy" flick was the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.640
we could see where Groot is in his ever-growing&nbsp;
state. It turns out that we get two iterations for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.380
the price of one. Initially, Groot is in his young&nbsp;
adult phase, and he remains that way for the bulk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.120
of the movie. However, in one of the end credit&nbsp;
scenes, we're treated to a super-sized version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.800
the character. Whether he's a happy-go-lucky&nbsp;
sapling or a living tree, gauging where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.427
character is during his life cycle has become a&nbsp;
fun revelation with each Guardian's appearances.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:37.380
Looking back, Thanos was a pretty incredible MCU&nbsp;
antagonist. Building up a multi-movie villain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.480
delivering a satisfying pay-off is hard. One-off&nbsp;
villains often outshine them, especially with good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.760
writing and execution. Past big bads, like Michael&nbsp;
B. Jordan’s Killmonger and Cate Blanchett’s Hela,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:53.700
exemplify this, and so does Chukwudi Iwuji’s High&nbsp;
Evolutionary in "Guardians of the Galaxy: Vol. 3."

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.380
The High Evolutionary is a detached and&nbsp;
lofty villain who fascinates and horrifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:02.280
all at once. While less sympathetic&nbsp;
than other antagonists, the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:06.720
is frightening in a cold and rational way&nbsp;
that makes him the stuff of nightmares.

00:10:06.863 --> 00:10:11.723
"I'm not trying to conquer the&nbsp;
universe. I'm perfecting it."

00:10:11.723 --> 00:10:16.740
Iwuji brings The High Evolutionary to life in an&nbsp;
incredibly believable way, with a performance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:21.840
ranks amongst the best that the MCU has given us&nbsp;
in years. The High Evolutionary may go down with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.180
his planet-sized ship, but he made a big impact&nbsp;
in what just might be his only MCU appearance.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.000
“Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3” may seem like a&nbsp;
blast from the past, but it technically belongs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.860
to Phase 5 of the MCU. It fits squarely in&nbsp;
the middle of the current mega-narrative arc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:42.720
the "Multiverse Saga." True, the third film in&nbsp;
the series doesn’t have much to do with traversing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.100
multiple dimensions, but it prepares the ground&nbsp;
for the increasingly complex storytelling ahead.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.740
Gamora’s story illustrates this. In “Vol. 3,”&nbsp;
the Daughter of Thanos is not herself. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.120
she's a past iteration of Gamora from&nbsp;
2014's "Guardians of the Galaxy" storyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.800
transplanted several years into the future.&nbsp;
The original Gamora died in "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.410
Infinity War" at the hands of her adopted father.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.300
"Everyone else who died in the past stayed dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.940
not her. Why? Was it the&nbsp;
magic cliff? I don't know."

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.320
The interactions between this "reset"&nbsp;
Gamora and the rest of the Guardians are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.820
a nice testing ground to explore how to tell&nbsp;
meaningful multiverse narratives that actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.720
matter. Of course, the fact that the Gamora&nbsp;
storyline was already teased in "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.980
Endgame" made it something fans were invested&nbsp;
in watching play out as soon as possible.

